abstract: >-
  Decentralized navigation for teams of trotting quadrupeds that accounts for the gait phase. A
  Bezier-curve trot controller for the simulated Unitree Go1 exposes its gait phase, and a measured
  capability map predicts how strongly a velocity change destabilizes the robot at each phase. The swarm
  layer solves reciprocal collision avoidance (ORCA) one gait period ahead, holds each non-urgent change
  for at most half a gait period, releases it at the phase the map predicts is safest and shapes it with
  a gait-aware slew limiter; urgent conflicts bypass the hold. Controls show that planning ahead and the
  bounded hold produce the reduction in urgent avoidance events, while the phase choice adds a smaller
  reduction in body tilt. Includes all experiment scripts, raw results and the scripts that reproduce
  every table and figure of the paper.
